# ── Internal working documents ─────────────────────────────────────
# Specs, roadmaps and research notes live here and are NOT committed.
# Audit reports ARE committed (exception below): the Jury gate is
# enforced by those artifacts, and a governance trail that `git clean`
# can destroy is not a trail. Audit reports contain no secrets.
# Public documentation lives in docs/ and IS committed. See CLAUDE.md.
#
# NOTE: this excludes resources/* (the CONTENTS), not resources/ (the
# directory). Git will not re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded, so `resources/` + `!resources/audits/` silently fails to
# un-ignore anything. Do not "simplify" this back.  (Jury J-C10)
/resources/*
!/resources/audits/

# ── Dependencies ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/
.yarn/

# ── Secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Jury J-M5: key material must never be one typo away from a commit.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.keystore
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
.railway/
.vercel/
.npmrc
.aws/
# Defence in depth (J-n6). These must never appear in a project repo, and the
# host's home directory is itself an initialized git repo with no root
# .gitignore — so a future mis-rooted `git init` has one more line of defence.
.ssh/
.claude.json*

# ── Build output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Jury J-M5: these are ANCHORED. Unanchored `dist/` or `build/` matches
# a directory of that name at ANY depth, which silently swallows source
# files that happen to live in one. Add per-workspace paths here rather
# than removing the leading slash.
/dist/
/build/
/apps/web/dist/
/worker/dist/
/apps/mobile/dist/
.next/
.expo/
.turbo/
coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo

# ── Aligner sidecar (Python) ───────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
# ANCHORED for the same reason: bare `models/` would match
# worker/src/models/, an ordinary TypeScript source directory.
/aligner/models/

# ── Test fixtures ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Jury J-M6: golden-file fixtures must be public-domain only. Anything
# under a `restricted/` fixture directory is never committed; store the
# extracted Block[] snapshot and a content hash instead.
**/fixtures/restricted/

# ── Session / agent scratch ────────────────────────────────────────
.remember/
.claude/settings.local.json

# ── Logs & OS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# SPIKE A audio fixtures ARE committed (J23-M5). They were briefly excluded to save
# 1.6 MB on the reasoning that they are "regenerable for ~$0.001". Jury: **regenerable
# is not the same claim as reproducible.** TTS output is not deterministic, so
# discarding the audio removes the only route to independently re-check a measurement
# -- which is leg (c) of the admissibility test Jury set for this very spike. It also
# worsened an open finding (J22-M1, audio provenance covering 1 of 3 languages) by
# making the other two unrecoverable from the repository.
#
# 1.6 MB is the cost of being able to falsify our own result. That is a good trade,
# and the habit it corrects is the dangerous one: tidying is how evidence goes missing.

# SPIKE A: byte-DETERMINISTIC concatenations, excluded; their SOURCE is committed.
#
# `*-gt.wav` and `*-gtlong.wav` are `gt/words/<lang>/*.wav` joined with a fixed
# 300 ms silence by `groundtruth.py`. The word clips ARE committed (3.6 MB, 70 paid
# calls, SHA-256 in the manifest), so these rebuild bit-for-bit at zero API cost and
# `--verify` proves it. Jury's ruling that "regenerable is not the same claim as
# reproducible" turned on TTS being NON-deterministic; concatenation is not, so
# excluding these loses no falsifiability. 35 MB.
/aligner/spike-a/out/*-gt.wav
/aligner/spike-a/out/*-gtlong.wav

# SPIKE D: the OCR CACHE, excluded. The RESULT is committed; this is not it.
#
# `ocr-cache.json` (297 KB) memoises engine output so `run.py` need not re-run OCR.
# The evidence is `spike-d-results.json`; the cache is an optimisation, and the
# fixtures that regenerate it are committed.
#
# It is excluded for a second reason worth recording, because it is a genuine
# interaction between two agents' work in one round. `secret-scan`'s
# `bare-credential-token` rule had its 48-character cap removed (J29-m1) so that
# long generated tokens stop escaping. OCR output from this spike loses inter-word
# spaces — `thenorthernshore.Eachstation`, `CoastalDynamicsQuarterly` — which is
# 28+ characters, mixed case, adjacent to digits: *exactly* the shape the rule now
# hunts. Eight findings, none a secret.
#
# The scanner is NOT weakened to accommodate this. No SKIP entry was added, because
# a rule that stops looking at machine-generated text is a rule that stops looking
# where a leaked key is hardest to see. If OCR output is ever committed as evidence
# rather than cache, the scanner SHOULD fire and a human SHOULD look.
/aligner/spike-d/out/ocr-cache.json
